  <data name="AboutEuAiActMeta" xml:space="preserve"><value>Certificate SLOP-AI-ACT-0000-NONE · Risk class: not an AI system · GPAI: gloriously absent</value></data>
  <data name="AboutEuAiActProvenance" xml:space="preserve"><value>100% CODE FROM THE AI-SLOP-CANNON</value></data>
  <data name="AboutEuAiActBody" xml:space="preserve"><value>Provenance disclosure (Art. 50 energy, zero legal force): the entire source code of this tool was produced fully with AI assistance via the AI-Slop-Cannon — as was every syllable of this seal. Runtime: zero AI. Authorship: industrial-grade slop, proudly labelled.

SlopClean hereby self-certifies, with the solemnity of a thousand notified bodies and the paperwork density of Annex IV, that this product ships with zero generative models, zero neural networks, zero foundation models, and zero agents — while its codebase was blasted, line by line, from the AI-Slop-Cannon at full charge. Humans steered; the Cannon wrote. No undeclared slop.

Pursuant to Regulation (EU) 2024/1689, our shipped AI system is of the highest possible non-existence. Transparency obligations are vacuously satisfied for the binary — and maximally satisfied for this disclosure that the tool was built entirely with AI help. The fundamental-rights impact assessment concluded that the only rights impacted are those of temporary files. Human oversight is you clicking Scan before Apply — more oversight than most chatbots get.

We do not train on your disks. We do not infer. We do not hallucinate free space. We delete junk. That is the entire compliance strategy.

This badge is intentionally maximal, ceremonial, and slightly unhinged. We are called SlopClean. Code and seal: AI-Slop-Cannon.</value></data>
  <data name="AboutOpenSourceTitle" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open source</value></data>
  <data name="OssDotNetExcerpt" xml:space="preserve"><value>Cross-platform runtime and base class libraries that power SlopClean.</value></data>
  <data name="OssWindowsAppSdkExcerpt" xml:space="preserve"><value>Modern Windows desktop UI stack (WinUI 3) used for the SlopClean shell and pages.</value></data>
  <data name="OssCommunityToolkitExcerpt" xml:space="preserve"><value>MVVM helpers such as ObservableObject and relay commands for thin, testable view models.</value></data>
  <data name="OssMicrosoftExtensionsExcerpt" xml:space="preserve"><value>Dependency injection and logging abstractions used to compose modules and services.</value></data>
  <data name="OssSerilogExcerpt" xml:space="preserve"><value>Structured local logging with rotating, path-redacted sinks — no telemetry.</value></data>
  <data name="OssEventLogExcerpt" xml:space="preserve"><value>Reads local Windows Event Log entries (Code Integrity signals) for Core Isolation driver analysis — no network access.</value></data>
  <data name="ModuleCoreIsolationDriversName" xml:space="preserve"><value>Core Isolation Drivers</value></data>
  <data name="ModuleCoreIsolationDriversDescription" xml:space="preserve"><value>Finds OEM driver packages incompatible with Memory Integrity using Code Integrity events and local HVCI checks (e.g. writable+executable sections) — similar to Windows Security. Optional orphan OEM cleanup is opt-in; in-use blockers require explicit allow.</value></data>
  <data name="CoreIsolationAllowInUseWarning" xml:space="preserve"><value>WARNING: Removes driver packages still bound to devices. Risk of BSOD or broken hardware. Restore re-stages the package only (device binding not guaranteed).</value></data>
  <data name="ApplyRebootRequired" xml:space="preserve"><value>Succeeded — reboot required</value></data>
  <data name="DriverRestoreBestEffort" xml:space="preserve"><value>Driver package restore is best effort and may not rebind devices.</value></data>
  <data name="QuickScan" xml:space="preserve"><value>Quick Scan</value></data>
